r entities by personal delivery, overnight mail service, or (for those who consented in writing to\nL96@P2: such service method), by facsimile transmission and/or email as follows. Listing the judge here constitutes a declaration\nL97@P2: that personal delivery on, or overnight mail to, the judge will be completed no later than 24 hours after the document is\nL98@P2: filed.\nL99@P2: \nL100@P2: \nL101@P2: \nL102@P2: \nL103@P2: \nL104@P2:  Service information continued on attached page\nL105@P2: I declare under penalty of perjury under the laws of the United States that the foregoing is true and correct.\nL106@P2: \nL107@P2: Date Printed Name Signature\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.cacb.uscourts.gov/sites/cacb/files/documents/forms/F9036-1.1DeBNDEACTIVATE.pdf)\nciteturn1364view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1343view0\",\"id\":68}); Total lines: 32\nL0@P0: UNITED STATES BANKRUPTCY COURT\nL1@P0: CENTRAL DISTRICT OF CALIFORNIA\nL2@P0: In re: CASE NO.:\nL3@P0: Debtor(s).\nL4@P0: DEBTOR’S REQUEST TO DEACTIVATE\nL5@P0: ELECTRONIC NOTICING (DeBN)\nL6@P0: Debtor Electronic Bankruptcy Noticing (DeBN) is a voluntary program that enables a debtor to receive by email the orders\nL7@P0: and court-generated notices normally sent by U.S. mail to a mailing address. To deactivate electronic noticing, a\nL8@P0: debtor who has an active DeBN account must complete and file this form with the court.\nL9@P0: DEACTIVATION REQUEST\nL10@P0: I request deactivation of my electronic noticing (DeBN) account.\nL11@P0: DEBTOR’S NAME AND EMAIL ADDRESS\nL12@P0: My name is:\nL13@P0: My email address is:\nL14@P0: (CAPITAL letters only)\nL15@P0: Confirm email address:\nL16@P0: (CAPITAL letters only)\nL17@P0: My DeBN account number is (located in the subject line of all DeBN emails):\nL18@P0: Select one:\nL19@P0: I am the Debtor in this bankruptcy